From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 11:37:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] instrument-ledger-regex-drops-dotted-builtins: the numpy
 ledger reads functions named by a dotted module path

---
 docs/seam/measuring.md                 |  2 +-
 tests/performance/conftest.py          |  7 +++++--
 tests/performance/test_seam_budgets.py | 11 +++++++++++
 3 files changed, 17 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/seam/measuring.md b/docs/seam/measuring.md
index fbe5768..02b9155 100644
--- a/docs/seam/measuring.md
+++ b/docs/seam/measuring.md
@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ Every measurement drives the probes in `src/goosedove/excel/probes.py`: hidden w
 
 ## Counts, as tests
 
-**Python, `tests/performance/test_seam_budgets.py`.** Two ledgers per probe, both exact and both deterministic. `call_profile` lists the seam's own functions that ran and how many times: `Registered.run`, the prepared path, then `_validate`, `_numbers`, the probe, the result rule. `numpy_calls` lists the numpy C calls cProfile saw: `ndarray.copy`, `ndarray.sum`, `ndarray.all`, `ufunc.reduce`, `numpy.frombuffer`, `numpy.ascontiguousarray`. A range whose summary from the host says all numbers, or all text, takes no look at its kinds, so nothing numpy did appears on an accepted range in; a rejected range finds its first bad cell with `argmax`, and `ndarray.any` appears only where a value must be checked, the date paths. That second ledger is measured, not derived; a ufunc applied by an operator, such as the kinds compare, raises no cProfile event and is not in it. `tests/performance/test_seam_allocation.py` pins the peak of the Python heap during one warm call with `tracemalloc`, which sees numpy's data buffers and not the host's.
+**Python, `tests/performance/test_seam_budgets.py`.** Two ledgers per probe, both exact and both deterministic. `call_profile` lists the seam's own functions that ran and how many times: `Registered.run`, the prepared path, then `_validate`, `_numbers`, the probe, the result rule. `numpy_calls` lists the numpy C calls cProfile saw: `ndarray.copy`, `ndarray.sum`, `ndarray.all`, `ufunc.reduce`, `numpy.frombuffer`, `numpy.ascontiguousarray`, and a function numpy reaches through a Python wrapper by its last name, `numpy.count_nonzero`, `numpy.interp`. A range whose summary from the host says all numbers, or all text, takes no look at its kinds, so nothing numpy did appears on an accepted range in; a rejected range finds its first bad cell with `argmax`, and `ndarray.any` appears only where a value must be checked, the date paths. That second ledger is measured, not derived; a ufunc applied by an operator, such as the kinds compare, raises no cProfile event and is not in it. `tests/performance/test_seam_allocation.py` pins the peak of the Python heap during one warm call with `tracemalloc`, which sees numpy's data buffers and not the host's.
 
 **Host, `tests/performance/HostWorkBudgets.cs`.** The host reports what it did to a `WorkCounter` the observer supplies; a worksheet call passes none, so the worksheet path builds nothing. Every increment sits at a boundary, never inside the cell loop.
 
diff --git a/tests/performance/conftest.py b/tests/performance/conftest.py
index beee002..a729fa0 100644
--- a/tests/performance/conftest.py
+++ b/tests/performance/conftest.py
@@ -55,9 +55,12 @@ def call_profile() -> Callable[[Callable[[], object]], dict[str, int]]:
 
 
 # cProfile names a C call like "<method 'any' of 'numpy.ndarray' objects>" or
-# "<built-in method numpy.frombuffer>"; these read the type and the name out of that.
+# "<built-in method numpy.frombuffer>"; these read the type and the name out of that. A
+# function a Python wrapper reaches is named by its full module path, such as
+# "<built-in method numpy._core._multiarray_umath.count_nonzero>", so the function pattern
+# takes any dotted path below numpy and keeps the last name.
 _NUMPY_METHOD = re.compile(r"<method '(\w+)' of 'numpy\.(\w+)' objects>")
-_NUMPY_FUNCTION = re.compile(r"<built-in method numpy\.(\w+)>")
+_NUMPY_FUNCTION = re.compile(r"<built-in method numpy(?:\.\w+)*\.(\w+)>")
 
 
 def _numpy_calls(fn: Callable[[], object]) -> dict[str, int]:
diff --git a/tests/performance/test_seam_budgets.py b/tests/performance/test_seam_budgets.py
